# Enforce LF line endings on every text file, cross-platform. The Windows CI runner
# checked out `.tks`/`.tkt` as CRLF (git autocrlf), so `teko fmt`'s canonical-LF output
# read those files back as non-idempotent and the fmt corpus tests panicked (issue #229).
# `text=auto` lets git auto-detect text vs binary; `eol=lf` pins text files to LF on
# checkout regardless of platform, keeping the corpus byte-consistent everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The committed host seeds (TRANSITIONAL — .31 only, removed in the first .32 wagon) are
# xz-compressed executables. `text=auto` above auto-detects binary content, but auto-detection is
# a heuristic and a seed whose bytes were rewritten as text is a seed whose sha256 no longer
# matches SEEDS.sha256 — i.e. a lane that refuses to provision. Pin them explicitly: `binary`
# expands to `-diff -merge -text`, so git never converts line endings and never tries to diff
# them into a review.
bootstrap/seeds/*.xz binary
